From e1b51d0e57ae928e4277f33735b296f56766bd80 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: seedds <seedds@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 06:05:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0154: HUD needle follower + e_mod follow when +0xa0 is NULL

Fixes the two #68 residuals (REPORT-HUD-68-ISSUES.md):

1. Compass needle double-shift (regression vs #67): the PicView match
   (MapMiniPlayer's All) is a FOLLOWER of the dial (MapMini's All), not
   a second dial. Its global is composed from the dial's by the game
   every frame (proven by #67: it tracked the drag with no slot-1 write
   at all). Writing the full compass target to it double-shifted it off
   the dial in #68 (log-55: slot 1 final_m3-pm_dx 346.8, the dial's
   shifted global). A follower now takes edx=0, edy=0, ek=1 (local
   unchanged) and skips the +0x70 write; the hide flag still applies
   (the S3 rule is NOT reverted -- that would bring back #66's
   needle-doesn't-hide bug). The global check is kept for followers:
   fix 2 makes e_mod converge, so the early return still fires on a
   steady frame and the write block does not re-run every frame.

2. e_mod stale by 30.8/32.8/10.4 (log-55): log-55 proved +0xa0 is NULL
   for every HUD All pane on device, so the 0150 local-matrix write
   never executes there and the game recomposes +0x70 from the
   +0x30/+0x48 components. The old global-check branch took the stale
   path when lm was NULL (e_mod never followed, e_orig did). Now
   lm==NULL is treated as a genuine base move and e_mod follows.
   Safe: the global check runs only when !recomputed (local unchanged,
   still holds our shift), and with no matrix the game cannot have
   recomposed from a stale one (the #65 hazard the lm distinction
   guards against). The +0xa0 matrix write is kept as harmless dead
   code; nothing in 0154 depends on it.

Also in this commit: CounterLife overlay rz 3 -> 0 (PicIcon, TxtLife)
is a separate deliverable in romfs-overlay/LayoutData/CounterLife.szs,
NOT in this patch. The runtime word table is regenerated from the
0154 source (1491 words, gcc 13.2.1; first word 0xf100003f).

Host verification (0154 mock, 51 checks): fail-before on 0152 code
reproduces both device symptoms (needle double-shifted off the dial;
e_mod stale by 30.8 after a base move), pass-after 51/51 on 0154.
Regressions: 0151 mock 44/44 (S3 follower + N5 parent move updated
for 0154; both fail on 0151 as designed), 0147 matcher 33/33, words
test 10/10, ASan+UBSan clean (the 0151/0147 harness leaks are
pre-existing pane_new allocations, identical on the old runtimes).
Device confirmation still needed: needle on the dial, e_mod gap 0,
life icon upright.
---
 dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c |  93 +++++++++--
 dm/games/0100000000010000/patches.py    | 195 ++++++++++++------------
 2 files changed, 177 insertions(+), 111 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c b/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c
index c057e48..2563358 100644
--- a/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c
+++ b/dm/games/0100000000010000/hud_runtime.c
@@ -109,6 +109,10 @@
 //   (no needle pane exists in MapMini); the matcher now takes an All with
 //   a PicView DIRECT child as compass (idx 0). MapPlayer's PicView sits
 //   under Icon00, so the full-screen map does not match.
+//   (0154) The PicView match is a FOLLOWER: MapMiniPlayer's All is a
+//   transform-child of MapMini's All, so it takes no local shift and no
+//   +0x70 write of its own -- only the hide flag. Writing the full target
+//   to it double-shifted it off the dial in #68 (log-55).
 //
 // (0137) Runtime HUD control for the in-game editor: the DISPLAYED
 // position is moved by writing the pane's GLOBAL matrix (+0x70) directly,
@@ -148,6 +152,14 @@
 // is never composed by the game" was wrong, and so was "the game
 // recomposes its +0x70 from the shifted local" -- it recomposes from the
 // shifted local MATRIX.
+// (0154) log-55 proved +0xa0 is NULL for every HUD All pane on device
+// (w3 bit0 set in all 521 edge-decision records), so the 0150 local-matrix
+// write never executes there and the game recomposes +0x70 from the
+// +0x30/+0x48 components instead. The matrix write is kept (harmless dead
+// code on these panes; it may matter for other pane types) -- nothing in
+// 0154 depends on it. The same NULL finding is why the e_mod follow path
+// below was dead (it required lm non-NULL); 0154 follows e_mod when
+// lm is NULL instead.
 // A moved pane's original LOCAL is stored in a small table (state +0x300,
 // 66 176-byte entries, eleven per element -- up to eleven layout instances can
 // match the same element) and the shifted local is always re-derived from that
@@ -855,6 +867,11 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
     // Element 4 (hints) and the white bar have no matcher entry: they stay
     // hidden in the overlay and are never moved by this runtime.
     int idx = -1;
+    // (0154) follower: an All matched via the PicView rule (MapMiniPlayer)
+    // rides its transform parent (MapMini's All) -- it takes no local shift
+    // of its own and no +0x70 write, only the hide flag. See the
+    // compensation and the global write below.
+    int follower = 0;
     if (nw[0] == HUD_E_ALL0) {
         // (0151) 0 compass: a 'PicMap' child (MapMini) OR a 'PicView' child
         // (MapMiniPlayer, the compass's separate layout: its All carries the
@@ -862,8 +879,19 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
         // in MapMini itself -- that is why the needle never hid in #66).
         // MapPlayer's PicView is NOT a direct child of its All (it sits
         // under Icon00), so the full-screen map does not match here.
-        if (hud_edge_child_named(pane, HUD_E_PICMAP0, HUD_E_PICMAP1, 0, 0, 2) ||
-            hud_edge_child_named(pane, HUD_E_PICVIEW0, HUD_E_PICVIEW1, 0, 0, 2)) idx = 0;
+        // (0154) The PicView match is a FOLLOWER, not a second dial:
+        // MapMiniPlayer's All is a transform-child of MapMini's All (its
+        // global is composed from the dial's -- log-55: its e_mod captured
+        // the dial's shifted global (346.8,-4) while its local is (0,0)),
+        // so writing the full compass target to it double-shifts it off
+        // the dial (#68). A follower takes edx=0, edy=0, ek=1 and skips
+        // the +0x70 write; the game recomposes its global from the dial
+        // every frame, which is also how it followed in #67 (when the
+        // 0x100 window skipped PicView entirely and there was no slot-1
+        // match at all). The hide flag still applies, which is what the
+        // S3 rule was added for.
+        if (hud_edge_child_named(pane, HUD_E_PICMAP0, HUD_E_PICMAP1, 0, 0, 2)) idx = 0;
+        else if (hud_edge_child_named(pane, HUD_E_PICVIEW0, HUD_E_PICVIEW1, 0, 0, 2)) { idx = 0; follower = 1; }
         else if (hud_edge_child_named(pane, HUD_E_LIFE0, 0, 0, 0, 1)) idx = 1;
         else if (hud_edge_child_child_named(pane, HUD_E_SHINE0, HUD_E_SHINE1, HUD_E_TSH0, HUD_E_TSH1)) idx = 3;
         else if (hud_edge_child_named(pane, HUD_E_ADD0, 0, 0, 0, 1)) {
@@ -1059,6 +1087,17 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
             }
         }
     }
+    // (0154) A follower rides its transform parent: the parent's shift is
+    // already in the follower's global (composed by the game from the
+    // parent's global), so its own local delta must be zero -- force the
+    // compensated values to the full target, giving edx=0, edy=0, ek=1
+    // below. The +0x18 parent here is the follower's own RootPane (no
+    // entry), so the lookup above already leaves pm_* at the identity;
+    // this force is what makes the follower explicit rather than
+    // accidental, and it also covers a future parent that does have an
+    // entry (the follower must never subtract a +0x18 parent's shift
+    // from a target its transform parent already applied).
+    if (follower) { pm_dx = tdx; pm_dy = tdy; pm_k = tk; }
     float edx = tdx - pm_dx;
     float edy = tdy - pm_dy;
     float ek = (pm_k == 1.0f) ? tk : (tk / pm_k);
@@ -1092,7 +1131,15 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
         if (*(uintptr_t *)(e + 0) == (uintptr_t)pane) { ent = e; found = 1; break; }
         if (!ent && *(u32 *)(e + 124) == 0) ent = e;   // first free slot
     }
-    if (!found && !hidden && edx == 0.0f && edy == 0.0f && ek == 1.0f) return;   // no-op: never take a slot
+    // (0154) A follower's effective delta is always the identity
+    // (edx=0, edy=0, ek=1), so without the !follower term this return
+    // would fire and the follower would never take an entry -- and then
+    // the hide flag could not be applied to it (the #66 symptom the S3
+    // rule exists for) and no match record would ever be logged for it.
+    // Taking the entry costs one of idx 0's eleven slots (the dial takes
+    // slot 0, the follower slot 1, as in 0152); the write block below is
+    // an identity local write plus the hide flag for it.
+    if (!found && !hidden && edx == 0.0f && edy == 0.0f && ek == 1.0f && !follower) return;   // no-op: never take a slot
     if (!ent) ent = (char *)table + idx * HUD_EDGE_ENTRY;   // all taken: reclaim the first (a reload)
     uintptr_t *e_pane = (uintptr_t *)(ent + 0);
     float *e_orig = (float *)(ent + 8);
@@ -1236,15 +1283,24 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
         // neither, the game rewrote the matrix itself: it is the new
         // original, so e_lm0 follows the live matrix and e_mod follows the
         // live global (which carries no shift, the game having overwritten
-        // ours). A NULL +0xa0 takes the stale path: nothing is followed,
-        // the write below still fixes +0x70. (The identity-transform case
-        // -- tdx 0, tdy 0, tk 1 -- makes lm_ours and lm_orig agree; lm_ours
-        // is tested first, which is correct there: with no shift to wipe,
-        // a global change can only be a genuine base move.)
+        // ours). (0154) A NULL +0xa0 is a genuine base move, not a stale
+        // wipe: this branch runs only when !recomputed, i.e. the local is
+        // unchanged and still holds our shift, and with no matrix the game
+        // cannot have recomposed from a stale one (the #65 hazard the lm
+        // distinction guards against), so a global change must be a
+        // genuine base/parent move and e_mod follows -- the old "NULL
+        // takes the stale path" left e_mod stale by 30.8/32.8/10.4 on
+        // device (log-55: the game moved some Alls' locals after first
+        // sighting and e_orig followed but e_mod did not). The
+        // identity-transform case -- tdx 0, tdy 0, tk 1 -- makes lm_ours
+        // and lm_orig agree; lm_ours is tested first, which is correct
+        // there: with no shift to wipe, a global change can only be a
+        // genuine base move.)
         recomputed = 1;
-        if (lm_ours) {
+        if (lm_ours || !lm) {
             // The game recomposed the global (a layout reload, an
-            // animation): the stored original global follows the game's
+            // animation), or -- lm NULL -- the base genuinely moved: the
+            // stored original global follows the game's
             // new value minus our applied ABSOLUTE delta (0142: the
             // global is absolute, so the reference is the absolute delta,
             // not the compensated *e_dx), so the next write re-derives
@@ -1270,9 +1326,9 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
                 e_mod[i] = *(const float *)((const char *)pane + 0x70 + i * 4);
             }
         }
-        // lm_orig (or a NULL +0xa0): a stale recompose wiped our write.
-        // e_mod and e_lm0 are left alone; the write below re-applies the
-        // shift to the local matrix and +0x70.
+        // lm_orig (lm non-NULL, holding the original): a stale recompose
+        // wiped our write. e_mod and e_lm0 are left alone; the write
+        // below re-applies the shift to the local matrix and +0x70.
     }
     // (0152) The early return also fires only when the match has been
     // logged (or logging is off): a pending log forces a re-run of the
@@ -1315,7 +1371,16 @@ void hud_edge(void *pane, const float *aspect_ptr, void *table, const float *par
     // the local write uses: the global already contains the parent's
     // transform, and compensating it again would move a child of a moved
     // parent one parent-shift short and one parent-scale over-divided.
-    {
+    // (0154) A follower takes NO +0x70 write: its global is recomposed
+    // by the game from its transform parent (the dial) every frame --
+    // proven by #67, where the needle tracked the drag with no slot-1
+    // write at all -- and writing e_mod + tdx here would double-shift it
+    // (the #68 regression: the needle landed at e_mod + tdx on top of
+    // the inherited shift). The local write above (identity) and the hide
+    // flag below still run. The global check above keeps e_mod following
+    // the dial's base (0154 fix 2), so the early return still fires on a
+    // steady frame and the write block does not re-run every frame.
+    if (!follower) {
         float *gm = (float *)((char *)pane + 0x70);
         gm[0] = e_mod[0] * tk;
         gm[1] = e_mod[1] * tk;
